"""Class 7 SST Ch 10 The Constitution of India - Hard-tier extras."""
from common import AR4, Q

AR = [
 AR4("The Constitution of India came into effect on 26 January 1950.", "The Constituent Assembly adopted the Constitution on 26 November 1949.", "b",
     "Both are true, but adoption on an earlier date does not by itself explain why the Constitution took effect on 26 January."),
 AR4("Dr B.R. Ambedkar is called the architect of the Constitution.", "He chaired the Drafting Committee of the Constituent Assembly.", "a",
     "His leading role in drafting is the reason for the title."),
 AR4("Directive Principles of State Policy can be enforced by courts.", "Directive Principles are guidelines that governments should try to achieve.", "d",
     "A is false, they are non-justiciable; R is true."),
 AR4("Fundamental Duties were added to the Constitution in 1976.", "They were added by the 44th Amendment.", "c",
     "A is true; R is false, it was the 42nd Amendment."),
 AR4("India is called a secular country.", "The State has no official religion and treats all religions equally.", "a",
     "Equal treatment with no state religion is the meaning of secular."),
 AR4("Members of the Constituent Assembly were chosen by the provincial legislative assemblies.", "The original Constitution was hand-written by Prem Behari Narain Raizada.", "b",
     "Both are true, but the calligrapher has nothing to do with how members were chosen."),
]

CASE = [
 Q("<b>Case study.</b> The Constituent Assembly first met on 9 December 1946 with Dr Rajendra Prasad as its President. The Drafting Committee under Dr B.R. Ambedkar prepared the draft, which members debated clause by clause for nearly three years. The Constitution was adopted on 26 November 1949. "
   "(i) Who chaired the Drafting Committee? (ii) When was the Constitution adopted? (iii) When did it come into effect? (iv) Why did the Assembly debate each clause for so long?",
   "(i) Dr B.R. Ambedkar (1). (ii) 26 November 1949 (1). (iii) 26 January 1950 (1). (iv) A constitution is the basic law for a diverse country (1); long debate let different views be heard so that the rules would be fair to all (1).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q("<b>Case study.</b> Rohan is told that every child aged 6 to 14 has a right to education, and that his school must treat all students equally before the law. His teacher adds that the government also tries to improve health and reduce inequality as goals, though no court can force it to meet these goals. "
   "(i) Which article gives the right to education to children aged 6 to 14? (ii) Which Fundamental Right means equality before law? (iii) Name the guidelines for government goals. (iv) How do Fundamental Rights differ from these guidelines?",
   "(i) Article 21A (1). (ii) Right to Equality (Article 14) (1). (iii) Directive Principles of State Policy (1). (iv) Fundamental Rights can be enforced by courts (1); Directive Principles are goals for governments and cannot be enforced in court (1).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q("<b>Case study.</b> India has a three-tier system of government: the Centre, the States and local bodies. The 73rd Amendment, passed in 1992, gave Panchayati Raj institutions constitutional status. A village in Odisha now elects its own gram panchayat to manage local needs. "
   "(i) Name the three levels of government. (ii) Which amendment gave Panchayati Raj constitutional status? (iii) Who elects the gram panchayat? (iv) Why is local government important in a democracy?",
   "(i) The Centre, the States and local bodies (1). (ii) The 73rd Amendment (1). (iii) The people (voters) of the village (1). (iv) Local people know their own problems best (1); it gives ordinary citizens a chance to take part in decisions (1).", "1+1+1+2"),
]

